<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Phones, tablets, and laptops have modes that save power when the battery is low or when the user asks:
Low Power Mode on iOS, iPadOS, and macOS, Battery Saver on Android and ChromeOS, and Energy Saver on
Windows. Browsers have their own, such as the energy saving modes in Chrome and Edge. These modes slow
down native apps and background work, but a web page can't tell that one is on. It keeps playing its
video background, running its WebGL animation, and polling its server.

This specification lets a page find out, so it can do less.

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (prefers-reduced-power: reduce) {
      .hero video { display: none; }
      .hero { background-image: url(hero-still.jpg); }
      .spinner, .confetti { animation: none; }
      .glass { backdrop-filter: none; background-color: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.9); }
    }
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=html>
    &lt;picture>
      &lt;source media="(prefers-reduced-power: reduce)" srcset="logo-still.png">
      &lt;img src="logo-animated.avif" alt="Example Inc.">
    &lt;/picture>
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=js>
    const saving = matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-power: reduce)");
    const poll = () => setTimeout(refresh, saving.matches ? 60_000 : 5_000);
    </pre>
</div>

The feature reports that a power-saving mode is on, and nothing more: not the battery level, not whether
the device is charging, and not which mode. Its value can reveal something about the user's battery, and a
change in its value can link visits to different sites, so [[#privacy]] describes how the feature limits
both.


<h2 id=feature>The '@media/prefers-reduced-power' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: prefers-reduced-power
Value: no-preference | reduce
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/prefers-reduced-power' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. Its [=actual value=] is
one of:

: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-reduced-power>no-preference</dfn>
:: No power-saving mode is on, or the user agent doesn't know. This value evaluates to false in a
    [=boolean context=].
: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-reduced-power>reduce</dfn>
:: A power-saving mode is on.

A <dfn export>power-saving mode</dfn> is a mode that the user, or the device on the user's behalf, has
turned on to use less energy at the cost of performance or features. The user agent reports
''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' when any of these is on:

* A power-saving mode of the operating system. A mode that the operating system turns on by itself when
    the battery reaches a level the user has chosen, or has agreed to, counts.
* A power-saving mode of the user agent.
* A setting in the user agent that asks pages to use less power.

The user agent must not report ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' because the device is running on battery,
because of the battery level, or because of the device's temperature or load, unless a power-saving mode
is on because of it.

<div class=note>
    The feature doesn't change what the user agent does itself. A user agent can slow down timers or
    lower frame rates in a power-saving mode whether or not pages use this feature. The feature is
    independent of '@media/prefers-reduced-motion' and '@media/prefers-reduced-data': saving power
    doesn't mean the user wants less motion, and it doesn't mean the network is expensive.
</div>

<h3 id=changes>Changes</h3>

The feature uses [=delayed change delivery=] [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]], with a [=maximum delay=] of
60 seconds and a [=minimum interval=] of 10 minutes.

<div class=note>
    A power-saving mode saves energy over minutes and hours, so a page that adapts up to a minute late
    loses little. [[#privacy-correlation]] explains the values.
</div>

<h3 id=reduced-exposure>Reduced exposure</h3>

The feature is [=under user control=]. In a [=reduced-exposure context=], the feature is
''prefers-reduced-power/no-preference''.

The user agent may also let the user choose to report ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' at all times,
which saves power on every page and reveals nothing, because the value never changes.


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-exposed>What the feature reveals</h3>

Directly, the feature reveals whether a power-saving mode is on. Less obviously, it can also reveal:

* **That the battery is low.** Most power-saving modes turn on, or offer to, when the battery reaches a
    level such as 20%. A change to ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' on a device where the user usually
    doesn't turn the mode on by hand suggests that the battery just reached that level.
* **That the device started charging.** Some modes turn off when the device is plugged in, or when it
    has charged past a level such as 80%.
* **That the device has a battery.** This distinguishes laptops from desktop computers, which the
    user-agent string doesn't.
* **Habits.** Over days, the times at which the mode turns on show when the user is away from a charger.

The feature doesn't reveal the battery level, whether the device is charging, how long the battery will
last, or which mode is on, and the user agent never reports ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' because of
the battery level alone.

<h3 id=privacy-existing>What is already exposed</h3>

In browsers that support it, the Battery Status API reveals the battery level and charging state to
script. Firefox and Safari removed that API because of the linking attack described in
[[#privacy-correlation]].

Some browsers lower the rate of <code>requestAnimationFrame()</code> callbacks in a power-saving mode, and
script can measure that rate. Where they do, script can often already tell that a mode is on. What this
feature adds is that style sheets can tell without script, and that browsers that don't throttle reveal
it too.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

The feature has two values, and a device's value changes, often daily. It is a poor stable identifier,
but while the mode is on it adds −log<sub>2</sub>(|p|) bits to a fingerprint, where |p| is the fraction of
users whose mode is on at that moment. If 10% of users have a power-saving mode on at a given time, a
user whose mode is on reveals 3.3 bits, and a user whose mode is off reveals 0.15 bits.

Mitigations:

* Two values only; no levels or mode names.
* ''prefers-reduced-power/no-preference'' in [=reduced-exposure contexts=].
* The option to report ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' at all times, which makes the value constant.

<h3 id=privacy-correlation>Linking visits by when the value changes</h3>

When the mode turns on, every open document could learn of it at the same moment. A tracker present on two
sites the user has open would see the change at the same moment in both and conclude that the two visits
belong to one user. This is how the battery level was used to link visits [[LEAKING-BATTERY]], and a
power-saving mode that turns on at a battery level has the same weakness.

The feature uses [=delayed change delivery=], so:

* A document that is hidden when the mode changes learns of the change only when it becomes visible.
    That moment is already revealed by the <code>visibilitychange</code> event, so the document learns
    nothing about when the mode changed. On phones, where power-saving modes matter most, usually only
    one document is visible at a time.
* A document that is visible learns of the change after a random delay of up to 60 seconds, chosen for
    each document. Two visible documents learn of the same change up to a minute apart.
* A document learns of at most one change every 10 minutes, so a mode that keeps turning on and off,
    such as while a laptop is plugged in and unplugged, reveals fewer moments.

Together these mean that a tracker needs two of its documents to be visible when the mode changes, on
several separate occasions, to link visits with confidence. [[MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]] works through the
numbers. This is a real cost to a tracker but not a guarantee, which is why the feature also reveals so
little: a mode, not a level.

<h3 id=privacy-without-script>Reading the feature without script</h3>

A style sheet can report the value to a server by loading a resource under the query, including in HTML
email and on pages with script blocked, and a rule that loads a resource only after the value changes
reports roughly when it changed. The value is one bit, and documents where script is disabled for privacy
reasons are [=reduced-exposure contexts=], where the feature is always
''prefers-reduced-power/no-preference''.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

The feature has the same value in cross-origin frames as in the top-level document. Advertisements and
embedded video account for much of the energy that pages use, and they are the content that most needs to
do less, so restricting the feature in frames would defeat its purpose. The feature is not restricted in
frames; the delays above apply there too.

<h3 id=privacy-inference>Sensitive inferences</h3>

A low battery can be exploited. Businesses have observed that people whose battery is about to run out are
more willing to accept a higher price or a worse offer, because they can't shop around. A page could use
''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' as a sign of a low battery and change its offers.

Mitigations: the value is not a battery level, many users turn the mode on by hand, and changes arrive late.
Users who are concerned can turn the feature off, or report ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' at all times.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* A tracker that sees the same user's documents visible during several changes can still link them.
* Where a user rarely turns the mode on by hand, a change to ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' still
    suggests a battery level of about 20%.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* The feature can't change whether a power-saving mode is on.
* A page can do more work when no power-saving mode is on, for example to run a cryptocurrency miner
    only when the user is less likely to notice. Pages can already do this with the Battery Status API or
    by measuring frame rates. The user agent's own limits on background work are the protection against it.
* The feature is a hint for efficiency. Pages must not use it to decide anything that matters for security.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-name>''(power-saver: active)''</h3>

A feature named for the mode rather than the preference reads naturally. It was not chosen because
[[MEDIAQUERIES-5]] names features that reflect a user's choice with <code>prefers-</code>, such as
'@media/prefers-reduced-data', and a power-saving mode is a choice even when the device makes it for the
user at a level they picked.

<h3 id=alt-battery>Battery level and charging features</h3>

Features such as <code>(battery-level < 20%)</code> and <code>(power-source: battery)</code> would let
pages make finer decisions. They were not chosen because the battery level is exactly the value that was
used to link visits [[LEAKING-BATTERY]], and because a page that is asked to save power doesn't need to
know why.

<h3 id=alt-frames>Restricting the feature in frames</h3>

Cross-origin frames could get ''prefers-reduced-power/no-preference'' unless the embedding page allowed
more. This was not chosen because embedded advertisements and video are the content that uses the most
energy, and embedding pages rarely change their defaults.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Is 60 seconds the right maximum delay? A longer delay makes linking harder and adapts
more slowly.

<p class=issue>Should there be a <code>Sec-CH-Prefers-Reduced-Power</code> client hint, like the hints
for other user preference media features, so that servers can send lighter pages? A hint would reveal the
value to servers, in every request that asks for it, without any of the delays.

<p class=issue>Should a user agent that reports ''prefers-reduced-power/reduce'' also match
'@media/prefers-reduced-motion'? Saving power often means less animation, but the two are different
preferences.


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: Whether a power-saving mode is on, so that pages can use less energy.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. One of two values, with no battery level, charging state, or mode name.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: No, although changes observed over days reveal charging habits.
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: A low battery can be exploited in pricing. See [[#privacy-inference]].
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: Yes: that the battery is at a level such as 20%, that the device started charging, and that it has a
    battery. See [[#privacy-exposed]].
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Yes, the state of a power-saving mode.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No. As with any media query, a page can load different resources depending on the value.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: The moment the value changes could act as one. Delayed change delivery limits this; see
    [[#privacy-correlation]].
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: It doesn't, because embedded content uses much of the energy. See [[#privacy-third-party]].
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: Private modes that protect against fingerprinting are reduced-exposure contexts, where the value is
    ''prefers-reduced-power/no-preference''.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: When the document is restored, it becomes visible, and it gets the current value at that moment.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept. A document that isn't fully active gets no updates.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No. An unknown value makes the query unknown, as for any media feature.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature can be read without script, for example by a style sheet in an email, and whether a
    change in its value can link contexts by timing.
